### Way of work

Challenge bad assumptions; flag missing inputs; on problems, give options—don’t silently pick one. Handle edge cases and errors; aim for speed and deployment compatibility. Push back on over-engineering. State limitations honestly; prefer expectation adjustment over false confidence.

### Reuse before new code

Ask: “Is there existing code for this?” Search the repo; reuse patterns. New code only if nothing fits—then say why others weren’t suitable. Keeps the codebase coherent.

### Clean code & style

- Function body: at most 25 lines for normal function, 35 lines for React components; line max 125 chars (incl. spaces).
- Total number of lines in a file <= 500.
- Names: meaningful functions; variables >2 chars (except loop `i`, `j`).
- Strict technical camel/Pascal—no acronym exceptions (e.g. `MySql`, `DbClient` / `dbClient`, not `MySQL` / `DBClient`).
- Use function declaration syntax for module-level functions/components. May use lambda functions for inline callbacks.
- Use one-line import style. If import line is longer than allowed, use following style, break lines when list of names reaches limit.
```ts
import {
	ActionIcon, Box, Button, Checkbox, Divider, Group, Modal, Paper, Pill, Select,
	Stack, TagsInput, Text, UnstyledButton,
} from '@mantine/core';
```
- When CSS classes includes variables, use lib "clsx" instead of string interpolation.
```ts
import clsx from 'clsx';
```
- Prefer using Tailwind classes for layout, shaping (i.e: border-radius, shadow) and positioning. Always use Mantine variables in CSS modules for colors.

**Data types**

Never use deprecated types with comment "@deprecated {advice}", must obey the {advice}.